1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,567
On the seventeenth day of the war at Kurukshetra, a chariot wheel sank into the mud.

2
00:00:06,017 --> 00:00:10,040
The greatest archer on the Kaurava side jumped down to free it.

3
00:00:10,040 --> 00:00:15,212
And while he was down there, struggling with the wheel, Arjuna's arrow found him.

4
00:00:15,662 --> 00:00:16,993
His name was Karna.

5
00:00:16,993 --> 00:00:22,105
And here is the cruel part: Arjuna had just killed his own elder brother.

6
00:00:22,555 --> 00:00:23,886
Arjuna didn't know.

7
00:00:23,886 --> 00:00:24,868
But Karna did.

8
00:00:24,868 --> 00:00:26,479
He had known for weeks.

9
00:00:26,479 --> 00:00:38,534
Because before the war began, Krishna himself had offered Karna everything: a throne, five brothers, and a place at the head of the family that had mocked him all his life.

10
00:00:38,984 --> 00:00:40,413
Karna said no.

11
00:00:40,863 --> 00:00:44,874
Today on What If Itihaas, we ask: what if he had said yes?

12
00:00:48,324 --> 00:01:01,593
Long before the war, a young princess named Kunti served the short-tempered sage Durvasa so well that he gave her a gift: a mantra that could call down any god and give her a child by him.

13
00:01:02,043 --> 00:01:03,987
Kunti was still unmarried.

14
00:01:03,987 --> 00:01:05,483
And she was curious.

15
00:01:05,483 --> 00:01:08,998
So she tested the mantra on Surya, the sun god.

16
00:01:09,448 --> 00:01:16,295
The child was born wearing golden armour and earrings, fused to his body: the kavacha and kundala.

17
00:01:16,295 --> 00:01:21,885
Terrified of scandal, Kunti placed the baby in a basket and set it on the river.

18
00:01:22,335 --> 00:01:27,480
The basket was found by Adhiratha, a charioteer, and his wife Radha.

19
00:01:27,480 --> 00:01:29,976
They raised the boy as their own.

20
00:01:29,976 --> 00:01:31,792
They named him Vasusena.

21
00:01:31,792 --> 00:01:36,180
The world would call him Radheya, son of Radha, and Karna.

22
00:01:36,630 --> 00:01:39,942
Remember those two names: Adhiratha and Radha.

23
00:01:39,942 --> 00:01:41,238
They matter later.

24
00:01:41,688 --> 00:01:43,716
Karna wanted to be a warrior.

25
00:01:43,716 --> 00:01:48,053
But the great teachers trained princes, not charioteers' sons.

26
00:01:48,053 --> 00:01:53,998
So he went to Parashurama, a teacher who only taught brahmins, and claimed to be one.

27
00:01:54,448 --> 00:01:59,326
One afternoon, Parashurama fell asleep with his head on Karna's lap.

28
00:01:59,326 --> 00:02:02,052
An insect bit deep into Karna's thigh.

29
00:02:02,052 --> 00:02:07,504
Rather than wake his teacher, Karna sat perfectly still while the blood ran.

30
00:02:07,954 --> 00:02:12,611
When Parashurama woke, he knew: no brahmin could bear pain like that.

31
00:02:12,611 --> 00:02:14,028
Only a warrior could.

32
00:02:14,028 --> 00:02:19,900
And he cursed his student: at the moment you need my teaching most, you will forget it.

33
00:02:20,350 --> 00:02:29,935
Years later, at a tournament in Hastinapura, Karna walked into the arena and matched every feat of Arjuna, the prince everyone adored.

34
00:02:29,935 --> 00:02:32,510
Then he challenged Arjuna to a duel.

35
00:02:32,960 --> 00:02:39,588
But a duel with a prince was for royalty, and Karna was asked to name his father and his lineage.

36
00:02:39,588 --> 00:02:41,707
He had no royal answer to give.

37
00:02:42,157 --> 00:02:46,700
And in that moment of humiliation, one prince stepped forward.

38
00:02:46,700 --> 00:02:52,269
Duryodhana, the eldest of the Kauravas, made Karna king of Anga on the spot.

39
00:02:52,719 --> 00:02:56,335
Duryodhana did not buy Karna's loyalty with gold.

40
00:02:56,335 --> 00:02:58,254
He bought it with dignity.

41
00:02:58,254 --> 00:02:59,951
And Karna never forgot.

42
00:03:00,401 --> 00:03:03,008
We should be honest about Karna, too.

43
00:03:03,008 --> 00:03:10,828
In the dice game, when Draupadi was dragged into the hall, Karna was among those who insulted her most cruelly.

44
00:03:10,828 --> 00:03:14,843
The epic doesn't hide his dark side, and neither will we.

45
00:03:15,293 --> 00:03:21,898
Karna was famous for one thing above all: he never refused anyone who asked him for a gift.

46
00:03:21,898 --> 00:03:29,736
So the god Indra, Arjuna's father, came to him disguised as a brahmin and asked for his armour and earrings.

47
00:03:30,186 --> 00:03:32,376
Karna knew exactly who he was.

48
00:03:32,376 --> 00:03:36,391
He cut the armour from his own body and gave it anyway.

49
00:03:36,391 --> 00:03:44,202
In return, Indra gave him a single divine weapon, the Shakti, that could kill any one enemy, but only once.

50
00:03:44,652 --> 00:03:50,989
Then, just before the war, Krishna came to Hastinapura to make one last attempt at peace.

51
00:03:50,989 --> 00:03:51,700
It failed.

52
00:03:51,700 --> 00:03:56,684
As he left, he invited Karna into his chariot and told him the secret.

53
00:03:57,134 --> 00:03:59,311
You are Kunti's firstborn son.

54
00:03:59,311 --> 00:04:02,068
By dharma, you are the eldest Pandava.

55
00:04:02,068 --> 00:04:03,012
Come with me.

56
00:04:03,012 --> 00:04:06,640
Your five brothers will honour you as their elder.

57
00:04:06,640 --> 00:04:08,526
The kingdom will be yours.

58
00:04:08,976 --> 00:04:10,796
Think about what that meant.

59
00:04:10,796 --> 00:04:13,657
Karna would rank above Yudhishthira himself.

60
00:04:13,657 --> 00:04:17,232
Everything the Pandavas were fighting for would be his.

61
00:04:17,682 --> 00:04:18,673
Karna said no.

62
00:04:18,673 --> 00:04:22,778
His reasons are some of the most moving lines in the epic.

63
00:04:22,778 --> 00:04:29,077
Radha and Adhiratha were his real parents; they had loved him when his mother let him go.

64
00:04:29,077 --> 00:04:35,517
Duryodhana had trusted him for years, and he would not betray that trust on the eve of war.

65
00:04:35,967 --> 00:04:40,433
And he asked Krishna to keep the secret, because he knew Yudhishthira.

66
00:04:40,433 --> 00:04:46,048
If Yudhishthira learned that Karna was his elder brother, he would hand him the kingdom.

67
00:04:46,048 --> 00:04:50,132
And Karna, out of loyalty, would hand it straight to Duryodhana.

68
00:04:50,582 --> 00:04:52,761
Then Kunti herself came to him.

69
00:04:52,761 --> 00:04:56,204
Karna refused her too, but he made her a promise.

70
00:04:56,204 --> 00:04:59,507
I will not kill four of your sons, only Arjuna.

71
00:04:59,507 --> 00:05:02,529
Whatever happens, you will still have five.

72
00:05:02,979 --> 00:05:10,280
In the war, Karna sat out the first ten days, after the elder statesman Bhishma publicly belittled him.

73
00:05:10,280 --> 00:05:14,179
He only entered on day eleven, once Bhishma had fallen.

74
00:05:14,629 --> 00:05:16,730
He kept his promise to Kunti.

75
00:05:16,730 --> 00:05:23,397
More than once he had Yudhishthira, Bhima, Nakula or Sahadeva at his mercy, and let them go.

76
00:05:23,847 --> 00:05:32,786
On the fourteenth night, he was forced to spend Indra's one-shot weapon on Bhima's son Ghatotkacha, the weapon he had saved for Arjuna.

77
00:05:33,235 --> 00:05:36,274
And on day seventeen, every debt came due.

78
00:05:36,274 --> 00:05:40,109
The chariot wheel sank, as a brahmin had once cursed.

79
00:05:40,109 --> 00:05:48,357
At the crucial moment the divine weapon Parashurama taught him slipped from his memory, as Parashurama had cursed.

80
00:05:48,357 --> 00:05:50,238
And Arjuna's arrow struck.

81
00:05:50,688 --> 00:05:54,454
Only after the war did Kunti tell her sons the truth.

82
00:05:54,454 --> 00:06:00,352
Yudhishthira was devastated: he had fought, and won, against his own elder brother.

83
00:06:00,802 --> 00:06:05,858
So here is our fork in the road: that conversation in Krishna's chariot.

84
00:06:06,308 --> 00:06:07,893
We change one thing.

85
00:06:07,893 --> 00:06:09,081
Karna says yes.

86
00:06:09,532 --> 00:06:17,078
From this point on, everything is speculation: our best reasoning from what the epic tells us about these people.

87
00:06:17,078 --> 00:06:18,748
Let's see where it leads.

88
00:06:19,198 --> 00:06:24,798
Duryodhana's whole war plan rested on one belief: Karna could beat Arjuna.

89
00:06:24,798 --> 00:06:27,901
Without Karna, his greatest hope is gone.

90
00:06:28,352 --> 00:06:29,696
Would he back down?

91
00:06:29,696 --> 00:06:30,616
Probably not.

92
00:06:30,616 --> 00:06:37,339
This is the prince who refused to give the Pandavas even as much land as the point of a needle.

93
00:06:37,339 --> 00:06:40,169
But his allies would see the odds shift.

94
00:06:40,169 --> 00:06:42,858
Some kings might quietly change sides.

95
00:06:43,308 --> 00:06:49,250
Remember Krishna's argument: by dharma, Kunti's firstborn is the eldest Pandava.

96
00:06:49,250 --> 00:06:57,196
That gives Karna a stronger claim to the throne than Yudhishthira, and arguably stronger than Duryodhana's.

97
00:06:57,646 --> 00:07:00,256
Now Bhishma and Drona have a problem.

98
00:07:00,256 --> 00:07:04,487
They fought for Duryodhana partly out of duty to the throne.

99
00:07:04,487 --> 00:07:10,553
If the rightful heir stands with the Pandavas, that duty becomes much harder to argue.

100
00:07:11,003 --> 00:07:17,595
If war still came, the Pandavas would field the two greatest archers of the age on the same side.

101
00:07:18,045 --> 00:07:19,873
Think of who might live.

102
00:07:19,873 --> 00:07:28,099
Abhimanyu, Arjuna's son, died inside the Chakravyuh formation; in the epic, Karna broke his bow from behind.

103
00:07:28,099 --> 00:07:33,354
Ghatotkacha was killed by the very weapon Karna had saved for Arjuna.

104
00:07:33,354 --> 00:07:36,477
In this timeline, neither attack happens.

105
00:07:36,927 --> 00:07:39,656
But this timeline is not a fairy tale.

106
00:07:39,656 --> 00:07:43,966
Draupadi has not forgotten what Karna said in the dice hall.

107
00:07:43,966 --> 00:07:45,834
Bhima has a famous temper.

108
00:07:45,834 --> 00:07:54,740
A family that suddenly gains an elder brother, one who insulted their wife in public, would not be a happy family overnight.

109
00:07:55,190 --> 00:07:55,862
And Karna?

110
00:07:55,862 --> 00:08:00,632
He would gain a throne and lose something he valued more: his own word.

111
00:08:00,632 --> 00:08:10,977
The man who never refused a request would have broken the one promise that defined him, his loyalty to the friend who stood by him when no one else would.

112
00:08:11,427 --> 00:08:13,475
So, what if Karna had said yes?

113
00:08:13,925 --> 00:08:15,833
Maybe the war is smaller.

114
00:08:15,833 --> 00:08:17,817
Maybe Abhimanyu grows old.

115
00:08:17,817 --> 00:08:21,328
Maybe Karna sits on the throne of Hastinapura.

116
00:08:21,778 --> 00:08:27,034
But some readers argue that Karna's "no" is exactly what makes him Karna.

117
00:08:27,034 --> 00:08:31,715
Krishna offered him dharma by blood; Karna chose loyalty by love.

118
00:08:31,715 --> 00:08:35,243
The Mahabharata does not tell us which was right.

119
00:08:35,243 --> 00:08:39,996
That's why we are still arguing about it after two thousand years.

120
00:08:40,446 --> 00:08:42,235
So let's argue about it.

121
00:08:42,235 --> 00:08:44,174
Was Karna right to refuse?

122
00:08:44,174 --> 00:08:47,379
Tell me in the comments: dharma or loyalty?

123
00:08:47,830 --> 00:08:53,312
And next time on What If Itihaas: what if Abhimanyu had escaped the Chakravyuh?

